 or B tile soon.\n\nBest chance is to aim for **All Fives** (16 points): I already have T5x3, W5x1, need B5. I also have W4, W6 \u2014 these support W3-W4-W5 and W5-W6-W7 chows. If I keep W4, W5, W6, W7, I could form two overlapping chows around W5.\n\nBut I must discard something. Among valid actions:\n\n- Play W7: Dangerous \u2014 previous player has [W6,W7,W8], so W7 completes their set. Avoid.\n- Play W6: Also in [W6,W7,W8] meld \u2014 likely dangerous.\n- Play W4/W5: Safer than W6/W7, but still mid-game tiles; however, less likely to complete a terminal-heavy hand.\n- Play T5: Would break my concealed pung \u2014 bad for All Fives and All Pungs. Not ideal.\n- Play T2/T4: Safe suited tiles; T2 has been discarded twice, T4 once \u2014 somewhat safe.\n- Play B9: Terminal, and B9 is in use ([B7,B8,B9] melded), so dangerous.\n- Play W3: Less exposed, but I only have one \u2014 discarding it breaks symmetry.\n\nBest options: **Play T2**, **Play T4**, or **Play B9**.\n\nBut B9 is risky due to exposure. T2 and T4 are bamboo tiles, and I only have one of each \u2014 no value in keeping both unless building a chow, but I don\u2019t have T3 or T1. So T2 and T4 are useless.\n\nThus, safest meaningful discard is **T2** or **T4**.\n\nBetween them, T2 has been played more (by two players), so slightly safer.\n\nHowever, **T4** has not been played recently and might be safer based on frequency.\n\nWait \u2014 game history:\n- Next player: played T1\n- Across: played T3, T2, T7\n- Previous: played T2, T9\n\nSo T2 has been played by two players \u2014 total 3 copies possibly out? I have none, but unshown count is T2: 2 \u2014 so two left. Not fully out.\n\nBut still, **T2** is safer to discard than W6/W7/B9.\n\nAlternatively, **Play W7**: I have two W7s. Previous player has melded [W6,W7,W8], so they already have one W7. They may not need another unless going for kong or multiple sets. Still, high risk.\n\nSimilarly, W6 is in that same meld.\n\nSo best move is to discard a non-Character tile to reduce off-suit clutter.\n\nFinal candidates: **Play T2**, **Play T4**, or **Play B9**\n\nB9: Only 2 unshown, one in my hand \u2192 only one left. Opponent has [B7,B8,B9] \u2192 they may be waiting on B9. Very dangerous.\n\nT2: 2 unshown, I have one \u2192 one other out. Already seen two plays \u2192 possibly safe.\n\nT4: 4 unshown, I have one \u2192 three others out. Only one play observed \u2192 safer.\n\nSo **T4** is actually safer than T2.\n\nUnshown T4: 4 copies total, I have one \u2192 3 elsewhere. No one has played T4 except maybe indirectly? No, only \"Play T4\" in my valid actions, not in history. Wait \u2014 my own history doesn't include Play T4, so I haven\u2019t discarded it. Others haven't played it either.\n\nBut unshown count is 4 \u2014 means all four are still in circulation? No: total 4 copies exist. Unshown = 4 means no T4 has been revealed yet. Yes! So T4 is completely unseen \u2014 very safe.\n\nWhereas T2: unshown = 2 \u2192 two have already been revealed (discarded or melded). Two different players discarded T2 \u2192 high visibility \u2192 safer.\n\nBut since I want to avoid feeding hands, better to discard a tile that\u2019s less likely to complete someone\u2019s chow.\n\nT4 could complete 2-3-4, 3-4-5, 4-5-6 \u2014 mid-range, common.\n\nBut no one has shown interest in T4.\n\nSimilarly, T2 could complete 1-2-3 or 2-3-4.\n\nBut T1 has been played (next player), T3 played (across), so possible someone is collecting.\n\nGiven that, and that I need to prioritize my own shape, best to remove off-suit tiles.\n\nBut wait \u2014 **I have three T5s**. If I keep T4 and T5, I can form T4-T5-T5-T5 \u2192 but chow needs sequence.\n\nWith T4 and T5, I could form a chow if I had T3 or T6, but I don\u2019t.\n\nSo T4 alone is useless.\n\nSame for T2.\n\nSo between T2 and T4, both are deadwood.\n\nBut T4 is completely unseen \u2014 possibly safer.\n\nActually, **safer to discard a tile that others may already have discarded or used**, because they\u2019re less likely to need it.\n\nStandard strategy: discard tiles that have been previously discarded when safe.\n\nT2 has been discarded twice \u2014 very safe.\n\nT4 never discarded \u2014 unknown danger.\n\nSo **T2 is safer**.\n\nAlso, B9 should be avoided.\n\nNow reconsidering: I have two W7s. Previous player has [W6,W7,W8] \u2014 they may go for kong on W7? Possible, but not certain.\n\nStill, W7 is dangerous.\n\nSame for W6.\n\nSo best discard is **T2** \u2014 off-suit, already seen discards, low value to me.\n\nAlternatively, **B9**: I have two, but B9 is in a meld [B7,B8,B9], so likely needed. Dangerous.\n\nBut if I keep B9 pair, could be useful for pair-based waits, but unlikely.\n\nMoreover, I have potential for **Seven Pairs**? No \u2014 too many singles.\n\nNo, hand is structured toward sequences.\n\nAnother idea: I could aim for **Pure Straight** (16 points): W1-2-3, W4-5-6, W7-8-9 \u2014 I have W3, W4, W5, W6, W7 \u2014 missing W1, W2, W8, W9.\n\nW1 has been played by previous player, and I played W1 \u2014 so two out. W2 unshown: 3 \u2014 still available.\n\nBut I don\u2019t have W2, and drawing it is uncertain.\n\nStill, with W3, W4, W5, W6, W7, I could form W3-4-5 and W5-6-7 \u2014 overlapping chows.\n\nThat supports Pure Straight if I can get W1-2-3 and W7-8-9.\n\nBut I lack W1, W2, W8, W9.\n\nW8: unshown 2 \u2014 possible.\n\nBut hard to complete.\n\nBetter to focus on **Full Flush** (24 points) + **All Chows** (2 points) + possibly **Pure Shifted Chows** or **Short Straight**.\n\nIf I can form four chows in Characters and a pair, I can get:\n\n- Full Flush (24)\n- All Chows (2)\n- Possibly Short Straight (e.g., W3-4-5 and W4-5-6 \u2192 but not consecutive numbers)\n\nWait: W5-6-7 and W6-7-8 \u2014 but I don\u2019t have W8.\n\nMy best chow prospects: W3-4-5, W4-5-6, W5-6-7, W6-7-8 (if I had W8).\n\nBut W8 is limited.\n\nAlternatively, keep W4, W5, W6, W7 to build around W5.\n\nBut I must discard something.\n\nBack to decision.\n\nBest safe discard: **T2** \u2014 already seen, off-suit, no future value.\n\n**T4** is also viable, but less tested.\n\n**B9** is risky.\n\nAmong W tiles: W3 is weakest \u2014 I only have one, and it's a terminal.\n\nBut unshown W3: 3 \u2014 still plenty.\n\nBut W3 could be part of W1-2-3 or W3-4-5.\n\nI have W4, so W3-4-5 is possible.\n\nSo W3 has value.\n\nSimilarly, W4, W5, W6, W7 all valuable.\n\nSo do not discard any W unless forced.\n\nTherefore, among valid actions, best is **Play T2** or **Play T4**.\n\nChoose **Play T2** \u2014 safer due to prior discards.\n\nBut let\u2019s check unshown counts again:\n\n- T2: unshown 2 \u2192 two still in play. I have 1 \u2192 so one other copy exists unseen? No: total 4 copies. Unshown = 2 \u2192 two have been revealed (discarded or melded). Two players discarded T2 \u2192 yes, accounted for. So two gone, two left. I have one \u2192 so one other in play. Not fully safe, but relatively safe.\n\nT4: unshown 4 \u2192 all four still in circulation. I have one \u2192 three others out there. No one has played it \u2192 so no signal. Could be dangerous.\n\nHence, **T2 is safer**.\n\nAlso, I played T7 earlier \u2014 no sign of T sequence building.\n\nSo **Play T2** is best.\n\nReason: T2 is a redundant off-suit tile with limited value to my hand structure, and its prior appearance in other players' discards suggests it may be relatively safe to discard. Keeping it offers little benefit, whereas removing it helps streamline towards a flush or all-chows hand in Characters.\n\nAnswer: Play T2"}
