+ one pung), needing only one more meld and a pair. More importantly, **Peng W7 removes a dangerous tile**: others are collecting high Characters (e.g., across has B7-B8-B9 and W6-W7-W8; previous has W6-W7-W8). W7 is hot, and keeping it in hand risks discard danger later.  \\n- Also, **no Chi options** now \\u2014 your only chance to act is Peng or Pass.\\n\\nReason:  \\nPerforming **Peng W7** secures a safe and useful meld, progresses your hand toward a potentially high-scoring **All Pungs** or **Half Flush**, reduces reliance on marginal chows, and avoids holding onto a risky tile (W7) that others may also be targeting. Although the immediate fan gain is limited (possibly only 2 points for **Pung of Terminals or Honors? No \\u2014 W7 is terminal**, yes! W7 is number 7 \\u2014 not terminal. Terminals are 1 and 9. So W7 is not terminal \\u2192 no 1-point pattern here). Still, the structural improvement and risk mitigation outweigh passing. There is no better action available.\\n\\nAnswer: Peng W7\"}", "tile": "{\"system_prompt\": \"\\n    You are a Chinese Standard Mahjong (\\u56fd\\u6807\\u9ebb\\u5c06, or MCR Mahjong) player. It is a four-player game, and it is played with a basic set of 136 tiles with Chinese characters and symbols.\\n    Players draw and discard tiles in turn until they complete a winning hand with a 14th tile. The basic type of winning hand consists of four melds and a pair, while there exist winning hands with several special patterns.\\n    **Tiles and Representation**\\n    * There are 4 identical copies of each suited tile and honored tiles.\\n    * Tiles are represented using Letter+Number, i.e. W7, T2, B4, F1, J3, etc.  \\n    * Suited tiles are divided into 3 suits: Characters(\\u4e07), Bamboos(\\u6761), and Dots(\\u997c), each numbered from 1 to 9.\\n    * Letter 'W' represents Character suit(\\u4e07),  'T' for Bamboo suit(\\u6761), 'B' for Dots(\\u997c), for example, W3 is Character-3, T8 is Bamboo-8, and B5 is Dot-5.\\n    * Honor tile